WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:07.120
When we think of clowns, several images come to&nbsp;
mind: Beloved entertainers who bring joy to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.000
children, fast-food mascots who you’d never&nbsp;
even consider trusting with your children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.280
and bloodthirsty, supernatural entities who are&nbsp;
probably planning to eat your children ... but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.200
even if there’s nothing you love better&nbsp;
than a red-nosed idiot in baggy clothes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.480
it’s hard to deny that clowns can be terrifying.&nbsp;
But for some people, this fear can be intense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.360
and they’d give a warmer welcome to the bubonic&nbsp;
plague than they would to a travelling circus. 
 &nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.160
This overwhelming fear of clowns is known as&nbsp;
“Coulrophobia”, taken from the ancient Greek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:46.560
word “Coulro” meaning “One who goes on stilts”&nbsp;
... so if you don’t already have a fear of clowns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.240
just take a few seconds to imagine a ten-foot&nbsp;
monster with a crimson smile looming over you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:59.840
You’re welcome. The term was first used around&nbsp;
1980, and although the World Health Organisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.600
doesn’t officially recognise it as a phobia, you&nbsp;
don’t have to look hard to find evidence for it.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:14.160
When the world’s oldest clown, Creeky Creekmore,&nbsp;
died back in 2014, the response on Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:19.920
and Twitter was mixed. And that’s putting it&nbsp;
mildly. There were many posts praising Creeky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.120
for all the charity work he’d done over his&nbsp;
ninety-eight years, but plenty of other people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.800
took the news as an opportunity to tell the&nbsp;
world just how much Creeky creeped them out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.520
and how much they hated clowns in general.&nbsp;
There’s just something about clowns that drains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.680
people of sympathy and fills them with terror.
 
Psychiatrists say that while true Coulrophobia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:47.440
is rare, it’s more common today than it once was,&nbsp;
and it’s not unusual for sufferers to go to huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.360
lengths to avoid being anywhere they could bump&nbsp;
into a clown. However, if they do accidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.840
wander into a circus and find themselves&nbsp;
face to face with their worst nightmare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:04.640
they may experience a number of physical symptoms,&nbsp;
like nausea, sweating, crying, screaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.960
even difficulty breathing – sounds a bit like&nbsp;
me after I listened to Radio 1 for ten minutes.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:17.440
But why clowns? What is it about them that freaks&nbsp;
us out? Well, Coulrophobia is thought to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.680
connected with the “uncanny valley” effect, where&nbsp;
we feel unsettled by things that appear human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:31.760
but are just a little bit “off”. Human-looking&nbsp;
robots, for instance, or ventriloquist’s dummies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:39.760
or zombies, or Michael Jackson. It’s the way&nbsp;
they move, or smile, or feast on your brains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:44.080
or grab their crotch and scream in the&nbsp;
middle of a song.It’s just not right. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.760
It interrupts the patterns your brain is used&nbsp;
to, making you feel uneasy, and it’s exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.360
the same with clowns. They have painted-on&nbsp;
smiles, but their real mouths might frown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.400
or scowl, or grimace, and your brain&nbsp;
just doesn’t know how to deal with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.160
Humans gather a huge amount of information from&nbsp;
facial expressions, and if that information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:11.440
gets smothered beneath a half inch of&nbsp;
grease paint, the system goes haywire. 
 &nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:14.880
Oh, and then there’s the red nose, of&nbsp;
course. What the holy hell is that all about?

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.640
Another theory is that the slightly “off”&nbsp;
appearance of clowns makes us think of death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.720
The face of a corpse, for instance,&nbsp;
looks almost like a sleeping face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:32.080
but somehow we can tell something’s different,&nbsp;
and that bothers us. And it’s not just clown’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.560
faces that make us whimper and crawl into the&nbsp;
foetal position, because humans are also very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.720
sensitive to body language. Clowns walk in&nbsp;
a weird way with their huge, floppy shoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.160
have an unusual posture, fall over a lot, and&nbsp;
can’t seem to resist cramming themselves into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:54.480
tiny little cars ... and it’s “inconsistent&nbsp;
stimuli” like these that are the problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:00.480
We’re hardwired to distrust the weird guy on the&nbsp;
bus, the drunk guy in the middle of the street,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.520
the maniac in face paint squirting water at&nbsp;
us through a flower. Anything that doesn’t fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.640
our normal view of the world is automatically&nbsp;
viewed as a threat, even if all it’s doing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.800
making balloon animals and laughing too much.
 
Coulrophobia also ties in with social anxiety,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:21.840
because we generally only see clowns in a social&nbsp;
context. Or at least, we bloody-well should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.720
do - if you stumble across one in your basement&nbsp;
then social anxiety is the least of your worries!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.640
Most people have a genuine fear of being mocked&nbsp;
by others or picked out from the crowd, but just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.680
how much of a part this plays in coulrophobia&nbsp;
isn’t clear. After all, this kind of social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.440
anxiety is common in other settings too - most&nbsp;
people would rather soil themselves than get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:50.160
up and go to the toilet during a comedian’s set -&nbsp;
but I’m fairly sure comedophopbia isn’t a thing.
 &nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.240
Whatever the cause of coulrophobia, it clearly&nbsp;
affects some people more than others. It’s thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:01.360
our fear of clowns stems from our childhood, which&nbsp;
is peak clown exposure time for most people..&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.040
According to psychology professor Joseph&nbsp;
Durwin, of California State University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.960
young children are very reactive to a familiar&nbsp;
body type with an unfamiliar face ... which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.800
is why hiring a clown to visit your sick&nbsp;
child could be about as sensible as hiring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.840
Colonel Sanders to visit your sick chicken.
 
That sentiment is backed up by perhaps the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:30.880
most well-known study of coulrophobia, which was&nbsp;
conducted by the University of Sheffield in 2008.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.200
Two-hundred-and-fifty children were asked&nbsp;
how they felt about a hospital ward being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:41.760
decorated with images of clowns. The results were&nbsp;
pretty clear, suggesting clowns were universally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:48.800
disliked by children, with some finding them&nbsp;
“frightening” and “unknowable” ... So FYI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.920
‘hospital clown’ might not be the best line&nbsp;
of work to get into these days. Not that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.640
seems to have held Patch Adams back. When Robin&nbsp;
Williams plays you in the film of your life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.280
you’ve probably done something right.
 
 
 
The weird thing is, some people who never saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:09.600
a clown in their young lives still lose control of&nbsp;
their bowels every time they drive past a circus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.200
So what’s going on there? Well, evidence suggests&nbsp;
phobias can be learned from your parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.960
which might help explain why the fear of clowns&nbsp;
is still going strong today despite travelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:27.200
circuses becoming less and less common in many&nbsp;
parts of the world. Thanks for that, Mum and Dad.&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.240
It’s most likely, however, that in order to&nbsp;
create a truly delicious “Coulrophobia stew”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.440
your childhood experiences are mixed in with&nbsp;
various terrifying pop culture depictions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:43.120
clowns, peppered with nightmares, and then baked&nbsp;
for a couple of decades on a low heat until you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:48.240
thoroughly phobic. I mean, just think how&nbsp;
many weird clowns we’ve seen in films and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:54.960
TV shows over the years. We had 1988’s “Killer&nbsp;
Klowns from Outer Space”, in which alien clowns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:00.640
massacre their victims before cocooning them in&nbsp;
cotton-candy. We’ve had Heath Ledger’s Joker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.520
a character who had many dark layers to&nbsp;
him, not least because Ledger tragically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.800
died shortly after filming his portrayal. Oh,&nbsp;
and for any 1970s kids watching this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.320
we should probably mention Zal Cleminson,&nbsp;
guitarist for the Sensational Alex Harvey Band,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:22.560
who wore black and white clown makeup on stage,&nbsp;
along with colourful bodysuits. Despite the band’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.640
comedic overtones, Cleminson’s “happy-sad”&nbsp;
demeanour certainly gave the performances a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:34.800
hint of menace. And that’s before you throw their&nbsp;
version of Tom Jones’s “Delilah” into the mix. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.960
Benjamin Radford, author of the 2016 book&nbsp;
“Bad Clowns”, claims sinister clowns have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:47.360
not only existed throughout history -- including&nbsp;
Harlequin, the King’s fool, and Mr Punch --- but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:53.280
also suggests they’ve evolved into our modern-day&nbsp;
internet trolls. Just like their ancestors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.360
trolls abuse, tease, and play with people for&nbsp;
their own amusement ... and as someone who’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.200
read his fair share of YouTube comments,&nbsp;
I wholeheartedly endorse this theory! 
 
 &nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:09.360
We don’t quite know where the “evil clown” thing&nbsp;
started, but it certainly goes back hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:15.360
of years at least. There’s Edgar Allan Poe’s&nbsp;
story “Hop Frog”, which is based on a jester,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:21.760
and Leoncavallo’s opera “Pagliacci”&nbsp;
features several murderous clowns. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.960
Andrew Stott – professor of English at the&nbsp;
University of Buffalo – argues the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:33.600
first “scary clown” can be found in “The Pickwick&nbsp;
Papers” by Charles Dickens. In one chapter of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.880
book, Dickens describes a clown as having  
 
“a bloated body and shrunken legs – their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.560
deformity enhanced a hundredfold by&nbsp;
the fantastic dress – the glassy eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:49.840
contrasting fearfully with the thick white&nbsp;
paint with which the face was besmeared;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:56.320
the grotesquely ornamented head, trembling&nbsp;
with paralysis, and the long skinny hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.000
rubbed with white chalk – all gave him&nbsp;
a hideous and unnatural appearance.” 
 &nbsp;

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.240
Wow. Doesn’t he sound like fun? Perfect&nbsp;
for your five-year-old’s birthday party.
 &nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.080
Stott believes Dickens was inspired by Joseph&nbsp;
Grimaldi, the “father of modern clowning”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.160
which makes sense because he edited&nbsp;
Grimaldi’s memoirs around the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:24.000
Grimaldi created the character “Joey” in the&nbsp;
early 1800s and is thought to be the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.240
clown to wear white-face make-up, along with the&nbsp;
huge painted-on smile, brightly coloured hair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:36.640
and over-the-top costume. Grimaldi, however, was&nbsp;
much more loved than Dickens’ macabre creation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.840
In fact, annual remembrance services are still&nbsp;
held for him at All Saints Church in Haggerston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.920
East London ... so there’s another place&nbsp;
to avoid if you aren’t a fan of clowns.
 &nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:53.360
But how did we get from the much-loved Grimaldi&nbsp;
to our modern-day “evil” clowns? One person who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.360 --> 00:10:00.320
bucked the trend was Emmet Kelly, who based his&nbsp;
sad clown “Weary Willie” on Depression-era tramps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.360
with a painted-on smile that wasn’t a smile&nbsp;
at all, but a very distinct frown. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:11.200
there’s TV clown “Bozo”, who was very popular&nbsp;
with children of the 50s and 60s ... and maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:16.400
it’s a generational thing ... but just take a&nbsp;
good look at him. Don’t you get the feeling he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.440
could snap at any moment and start feasting&nbsp;
on your internal organs? Maybe it’s just me.

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:27.760
From Bozo, it’s easy to see a connection with&nbsp;
Ronald McDonald, that terrifying “Pied Piper”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.800
style clown, whose sole mission in life was&nbsp;
to lead children to a mystical mountain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:38.240
cheeseburgers in the Land of Coronary Artery&nbsp;
Disease. In the early twenty-first century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:44.480
it was estimated that more than 95% of American&nbsp;
children recognised him, which led to concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:50.240
about childhood obesity, and is one of the reasons&nbsp;
he doesn’t tend to appear in adverts anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:55.120
Well, that and the fact that he’s clearly a demon&nbsp;
straight from the Ninth Circle of Hell with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:00.320
terrifying death mask for face and a flagrant&nbsp;
disregard for the benefits of a balanced diet.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:06.480
One man who did nothing to help the reputation&nbsp;
of clowns was John Wayne Gacy, a notorious serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:12.240
killer who sexually assaulted and murdered at&nbsp;
least thirty-three young men and boys before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:18.880
burying them in the crawl space underneath his&nbsp;
house. In 1975, Gacy became a member of the “Jolly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:26.000
Joker” clown club and created two characters for&nbsp;
himself: “Pogo” who he described as a happy clown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:33.120
and “Patches” who was significantly more serious.&nbsp;
Gacy never carried out his crimes in costume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:38.160
but that didn’t stop the media dubbing him&nbsp;
“The Killer Clown” and giving Coulrophobes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:43.200
yet another reason to double check the lock on&nbsp;
the front door before going to bed each night.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:48.720
And of course, how could I make a video about&nbsp;
Coulrophobia and evil clowns without mentioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:56.000
the King - Stephen King - and his terrifying&nbsp;
creation, “Pennywise the Dancing Clown”. King’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.920
epic novel “IT” was published in 1986 and&nbsp;
has since passed into scary clown legend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:08.800
Mostly manifesting as Pennywise, IT is actually&nbsp;
an ancient cosmic entity, which awakens once every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:14.240
generation to feed upon the children of Derry,&nbsp;
Maine. It’s easy to draw a parallel between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:19.840
Pennywise and John Wayne Gacy, of course, but King&nbsp;
has never mentioned him in connection with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:25.600
character. In fact, King was originally inspired&nbsp;
by the children’s tale “Three Billy Goats Gruff”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.440
but rather than a troll under a bridge,&nbsp;
he wanted to write about some other kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:33.600
of creature inhabiting a sewer system.&nbsp;
And when he asked himself what scared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:38.480
children more than anything else in the&nbsp;
world, there was only one answer: Clowns.

00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:43.840
The book was followed by a 1990 TV&nbsp;
mini series starring Tim Curry as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:49.040
very “Bozo-inspired” Pennywise and the&nbsp;
recent films featuring Bill Skarsgard’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.800
equally horrifying portrayal. Oh, and just to&nbsp;
get your Coulrophobia juices flowing even more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:59.680
Pennywise comes out to feed every&nbsp;
twenty-seven years ... and the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:06.240
was made in 2017 ... twenty-seven&nbsp;
years after the original miniseries.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:06.960
You’re welcome.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:12.800
A year before IT hit the cinemas, clown&nbsp;
sightings suddenly became a very common and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:20.320
unnerving experience with the advent of “The&nbsp;
Great Clown Panic of 2016”, but “phantom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:25.760
clowns” have been sporadically spotted around&nbsp;
the world for decades. The 1980s saw multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:31.200
reports made by children claiming men in clown&nbsp;
suits had attempted to lure them into a van,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:37.600
which spread panic across the USA. And on the&nbsp;
other side of the Atlantic in 2013, a clown was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:44.240
spotted in various locations around Northampton,&nbsp;
standing perfectly still and silent, and generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:49.440
freaking people the hell out. It turned out to be&nbsp;
the work of three students having a bit of fun,---&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.440 --> 00:13:55.040
but unsurprisingly a bunch of YouTubers took&nbsp;
inspiration from the stunt, dressing up as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.040 --> 00:14:00.400
killer clowns and scaring the bejesus out of&nbsp;
the general public, gaining millions of views&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:05.920
in the process. I mean, what kind of YouTuber&nbsp;
would do something like that, just for views? 
 &nbsp;

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:12.960
The first of the 2016 sightings was&nbsp;
reported in Green Bay, Wisconsin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:18.080
in early August, when pictures of a creepy&nbsp;
clown in an empty parking lot went viral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:23.760
A Facebook page went up, proclaiming the clown was&nbsp;
called “Gags”, and although it later turned out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:29.360
be a publicity stunt for a horror film, the craze&nbsp;
had already started to spread. In South Carolina,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:34.320
a nine-year-old boy told his mother that two&nbsp;
men dressed as clowns tried to lure him into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:40.880
woods, and by October clown sightings had been&nbsp;
reported all over the States, Canada, the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:45.760
Australia, and in sixteen other countries. 
 
People were genuinely terrified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:53.360
President of the World Clown Association, Randy&nbsp;
Christensen, took a very public stance against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:59.520
the trend, not least because circuses and other&nbsp;
clown-related business were negatively affected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:05.280
Professional clowns intensely dislike the&nbsp;
“evil clown” persona for obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:09.520
and have no desire to add to the&nbsp;
growing issue of Coulrophobia. 
 &nbsp;

00:15:09.520 --> 00:15:13.840
It even got to the point where several&nbsp;
news outlets reported the possibility of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:19.760
“clown purge” or “attack” on the eve of&nbsp;
Halloween, which led to some Florida residents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:26.160
“packing heat” when they went trick or treating.
As it turned out, a widespread clown apocalypse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:31.920
never came, but one family was attacked on&nbsp;
Halloween night by twenty people in clown masks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.880
so maybe the guns weren’t&nbsp;
such a bad idea, after all.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:41.600
So, yes, the events of 2016 certainly weren’t a&nbsp;
great help to anyone suffering from Coulrophobia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:45.680
In fact, for them, it was possibly a worse&nbsp;
year than 2020 has been for the rest of us!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:50.080
But the important thing to remember is&nbsp;
that most clowns are completely harmless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:54.320
and there’s absolutely no reason to&nbsp;
be scared of them. No reason at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:59.040
Even if you're in bed one night, in the&nbsp;
dark, on your own, and you suddenly hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.600
the “flop” of massive shoes downstairs,&nbsp;
followed by the “parp” of a red nose. 
 &nbsp;

00:16:04.480 --> 00:16:11.760
Even if you’re busy recording a YouTube&nbsp;
video, and ... Wait, did you hear that?

